    <section id="rules" class="docs__section">
      <p class="docs__num mono">01</p>
      <h2>The rules</h2>
      <p>Canadian Highlander (Canlander for short) is a two-player constructed format. You build a deck from nearly any Magic card ever printed, and you play it against one opponent, starting at 20 life, with normal Magic rules.</p>
      <dl class="rules-table">
        <div><dt>Deck size</dt><dd>At least 100 cards. There's no maximum, but almost everyone plays exactly 100.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Copies</dt><dd>One of each card. Basic lands, and cards that explicitly allow any number (like Relentless Rats), are the exceptions.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Sideboard</dt><dd>None. Your hundred cards play every game.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Points</dt><dd>Each deck gets 10 points to spend on cards from the <a href="{{ site.official.points }}">points list</a>.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Banned</dt><dd>The <a href="https://magic.wizards.com/en/banned-restricted-list">Vintage banned list</a>, plus a handful of extra categories: ante cards, Conspiracies, cards with stickers or attractions, dexterity cards, Acorn and silver-bordered cards, sub-game cards, and cards Wizards has removed for racist depictions.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Restricted</dt><dd>Everything is one-of already, so restricted cards play like any other card.</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p class="aside-note">The Canlander Council maintains the official rules at <a href="{{ site.official.rules }}">canadianhighlander.ca</a>. If anything here disagrees with theirs, theirs wins.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="points" class="docs__section">
      <p class="docs__num mono">02</p>
      <h2>How points work</h2>
      <p>Instead of banning the strongest cards, Canlander gives {{ points.list.length }} of them a point value, from 1 up to Ancestral Recall at 8. Every other card is worth zero.</p>
      <p>Each deck gets ten points. Spend them on one big card or a handful of smaller ones. Every competitive deck uses all ten, so the real question is which pointed cards do the most for your deck.</p>
      
      <div class="cardrow">
        {{ mtgcard("Ancestral Recall", 8, "normal") }}{{ mtgcard("Black Lotus", 7, "normal") }}{{ mtgcard("Time Walk", 5, "normal") }}{{ mtgcard("Sol Ring", 3, "normal") }}{{ mtgcard("Strip Mine", 1, "normal") }}{{ mtgcard("Timetwister", 0, "normal") }}
      </div>
      <p>The costs are based on how strong a card is in this format, not how famous it is. That's why Ancestral Recall costs more than Black Lotus and Timetwister costs nothing. The list also changes a few times a year as decks rise and fall.</p>
      <p><a class="link-arrow" href="/build/#points">See all {{ points.list.length }} pointed cards and try spending ten <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></p>
    </section>

    <section id="playing" class="docs__section">
      <p class="docs__num mono">03</p>
      <h2>Playing a game</h2>
      <p>Games are one-on-one at 20 life with the normal Magic rules, mulligans included. If you've played any constructed format, you already know how to play.</p>
      <p>The big difference is variety. You only see a small part of a 100-card deck each game, so games go in more directions than they do in 60-card formats, and you'll run into cards you haven't seen in years.</p>
      <p>Match structure depends on the event, so check its rules. Your main deck plays every game, so build it ready for anything.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="building" class="docs__section">
      <p class="docs__num mono">04</p>
      <h2>Building a deck</h2>
      <p>Start with someone else's list. Canlander is a hard format to brew for, and a proven list has already solved the points, the mana and the matchups. Pick one from the <a href="/decks/">decks page</a>, the <a href="https://moxfield.com/users/CanlanderWinnersArchive">Winner's Archive</a> or <a href="https://canlandex.com/">Canlandex</a>, play it for a while, then start changing it. When you're ready to build from scratch, the <a href="/build/">build guide</a> covers spending points, choosing a plan, land counts and filling a hundred slots.</p>
      <p class="aside-note">The short version: spend all ten points on cards that fit your plan, play 32 to 36 lands, pack at least fourteen cheap interaction spells, and pick cards that are good against every deck, since your main deck plays every game.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cards" class="docs__section">
      <p class="docs__num mono">05</p>
      <h2>Getting cards</h2>
      <p>Your deck can be great without a Black Lotus. Every deck has the same ten points, so most of a deck's strength comes from its 90-odd free cards, and a lot of those are cheap.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Start online.</b> <a href="https://cockatrice.github.io/">Cockatrice</a> is free and has every card, so you can test a deck before buying anything.</li>
        <li><b>Use proxies.</b> The Discord webcam events are proxy friendly, and so are a lot of local groups. For paper events, check the store's proxy policy.</li>
        <li><b>Copy a proven list.</b> Starting from a winning deck means every card you buy has already earned its slot.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="glossary" class="docs__section">
      <p class="docs__num mono">06</p>
      <h2>Glossary</h2>
      <dl class="glossary">
        <div><dt>Canlander</dt><dd>Short for Canadian Highlander. You'll also see CanLander and CHL.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Highlander</dt><dd>Any singleton format, after the film tagline "there can be only one."</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Points list</dt><dd>The official list of cards that cost points to include.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Pointed card</dt><dd>A card on the points list. "Is that pointed?" means "does it cost points?"</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Mana denial</dt><dd>Cards that attack your lands or stop you finding them: Blood Moon effects, search hate like Aven Mindcensor, and Strip Mine and Wasteland. You'll see a lot of it.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>The Council</dt><dd>The volunteer group that maintains the points list and rules.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Power Nine</dt><dd>Black Lotus, the five Moxen, Ancestral Recall, Time Walk, and Timetwister. All legal here; most of them pointed.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Weekly</dt><dd>A recurring event, usually online over Cockatrice or Discord video.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>7-Point Highlander</dt><dd>Also called Australian Highlander. A different, 60-card format with its own list.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section id="faq" class="docs__section">
      <p class="docs__num mono">07</p>
      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Is Canlander an official Wizards of the Coast format?</summary>
          <p>It's a fan-run format with its own council and points list. Wizards has written about it and hosted events for it.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Should I build my first deck from scratch?</summary>
          <p>Not your first one. Canlander asks you to think about Magic in its own way: one-on-one at twenty life, low curves, cheap interaction, and a lot of mana denial. Grab a list from the <a href="/decks/">decks page</a>, play it, and change it as you learn what you like.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How often does the points list change?</summary>
          <p>A few times a year, on a roughly quarterly schedule. The council announces changes the day before they take effect and explains its reasoning in an article. You can read those on <a href="{{ site.official.articles }}">canadianhighlander.ca</a>.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do I have to spend all ten points?</summary>
          <p>The rules let you spend fewer, but competitive decks always spend all ten. If you don't own a pointed card yet, the Discord's webcam events allow proxies, so you can still play the full list.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Where do I find people to play with?</summary>
          <p>The <a href="{{ site.official.discord }}">Canadian Highlander Discord</a> runs weekly online events and is the fastest way to find a game. See <a href="/play/">Play</a> for the schedule and clients.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

